::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has side lengths 5, 5, and 8. How is it classified by sides?
{
=A. Isosceles#Correct. Two side lengths are equal, so the triangle is isosceles.
~B. Scalene#Scalene means no equal sides, but this triangle has two sides of length 5.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral means all three sides are equal, but only two side lengths match.
~D. Right#Right classifies by angle measure, not by side lengths.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has side lengths 4, 6, and 9. How is it classified by sides?
{
~A. Isosceles#Isosceles requires at least two congruent sides, which are not shown.
=B. Scalene#Correct. All three side lengths are different, so the triangle is scalene.
~C. Equilateral#Equilateral requires three congruent sides.
~D. Obtuse#Obtuse classifies by angle measure, not side length.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 35°, 55°, and 90°. How is it classified by angles?
{
~A. Acute#Acute means all angles are less than 90°, but one angle is exactly 90°.
~B. Obtuse#Obtuse means one angle is greater than 90°.
=C. Right#Correct. A triangle with one 90° angle is a right triangle.
~D. Equilateral#Equilateral classifies by sides, and equilateral triangles have three 60° angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 40°, 65°, and 75°. How is it classified by angles?
{
~A. Right#A right triangle must have one 90° angle.
~B. Obtuse#An obtuse triangle must have one angle greater than 90°.
~C. Scalene#Scalene classifies by side lengths, not angle measures.
=D. Acute#Correct. All three angles are less than 90°, so the triangle is acute.
}

::GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U03_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: Readiness Support Only
Question:
A triangle has angles 25°, 35°, and 120°. How is it classified by angles?
{
=A. Obtuse#Correct. The 120° angle is greater than 90°, so the triangle is obtuse.
~B. Acute#Acute requires all angles to be less than 90°.
~C. Right#Right requires one angle to be exactly 90°.
~D. Isosceles#Isosceles classifies by side lengths, not angle measures.
}

